# Tier hot loop (campaign host-expert-bank): the CPU expert dot in the
# quantised domain, ggml's approach, routed by call shape.
#
# The tier's native-format path (IQ3_XXS gate/up, IQ4_NL or Q8_0 down; IQ4_XS
# arrives in the IQ4_NL layout) dequantised each weight row to f32 and then
# ran a plain f32 dot. ggml instead dots in the quantised domain: the
# activation is quantised ONCE per stage to int8 with one scale per 32 values,
# and maddubs/madd do the arithmetic on the packed bytes.
#
# SHAPE ROUTING (the served gate's finding). The quantised dot is the decode
# kernel only: a tier call with <= 8 jobs (one routed token per expert) takes
# it; a prefill-shaped call (an expert with many routed tokens) keeps the old
# f32 decode + the one-job-per-lane AVX2 dot, which is the better prefill
# kernel. The first form of this patch applied the quantised dot to every
# shape and measured prefill 68.4 -> 49.5 t/s (dense, 20,085 tokens, B60)
# while decode rose 6.0 -> 6.6. With the shape routing: prefill 63.1 t/s
# (base 64.2) and decode 6.5 t/s (base 5.9). The cut is a pure function of the
# call shape (DESIGN 3.4). MOE_CPU_TIER_Q8_DOT=0 restores the pre-0074 routing
# (A/B).
#
# Numerics change: the int8 activation replaces the f16-to-f32 decode, so the
# cells hold to a stated tolerance, not to bits. The tolerance cell compares
# against the f32 tier at the served shape; the shape-routing cell asserts a
# decode-shaped call equals the AVX2 path and a prefill-shaped call equals the
# f32 reference bitwise. The debug path found the one real bug --
# _mm256_sign_epi8 ZEROES the source where its sign byte is 0 (PSIGNB
# semantics), so the IQ3_XXS sign vector carries +1 for a clear bit, not 0.
#
# Files: moe_cpu_expert.hpp (table accessors, a test hook), moe_cpu_expert.cpp
# (the accessors, the shape-routed AVX2 dispatch), moe_cpu_expert_avx2.cpp (the
# quantised stage), and the unit test.

